  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Let $a$ and $b$ be natural numbers. If\n$$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt a+\\sqrt b}=2,$$\nthen show that $a$ is the product of consecutive natural numbers.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=k(k+1)$, where $k=\\sqrt b\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nA common denominator gives $\\sqrt b=a-b$, so $b$ is a perfect square.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt a+\\sqrt b}&=\\frac{(\\sqrt a+\\sqrt b)-(\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b)}{a-b}\\\\\\frac{1}{\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt a+\\sqrt b}&=\\frac{2\\sqrt b}{a-b}.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $\\frac{2\\sqrt b}{a-b}=2$, so $\\sqrt b=a-b$. Since $a-b$ is an integer, write $k=\\sqrt b\\in\\mathbb N$. Then $b=k^2$ and\n$$\\begin{align*}a&=b+\\sqrt b\\\\a&=k^2+k\\\\a&=k(k+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a$ is the product of consecutive natural numbers.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 72
  },
